n and evening going from one of  the four major areas to another will  trigger the time of day to progress  meaning the player cannot visit multiple  areas within the same time of day in a  single loop the eight visionaries the  player has to kill are spread out  through the four areas at the four  different times of day so if the player  can only visit four areas in a single  loop but needs to kill all eight in a  single run that's where the game's  murder puzzle comes in the crux of the  entire thing through investigation the  player has to figure out alternate  methods to eliminate the visionaries in  a single loop the gold and loop as dana  refers to it the end game if that seems  like a lot to take in well  that was kind of the issue a huge amount  of information was just being tossed at  the players and none of it was sinking  in so for dana it was back to the  drawing board but the only solution  being something that  went against her own design principles a  guided tour a linear path in which the  player's hands were lightly held through  all of its moving parts and pieces i was  initially resistant to the idea of  making this change because i really  loved how non-linear the game was and i  didn't want to back i didn't want to  give an inch  on that thing where it's like okay we  introduce you to this world and we say  go go wherever you want do whatever you  want being lost and confused is fun  because of the like the serotonin hit of  getting the answer but the game we had  made was a game where the player had no  idea when that eureka moment was coming  at that point in the game the way we had  design it is the thing to do next is to  go out in the world with no idea what  you're gonna find and just  stumble upon something for some players  that's absolutely satisfying and for me  that's absolutely satisfying i've played  games that work that way and i've loved  them  but in in this case it wasn't quite  working so what better solution than  creating a l